<th id="S1.T1.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;">Kernel estimate (MC)</th>
<td id="S1.T1.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(PN_{\mathrm{MC}}T^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>MC</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(PN_{\mathrm{MC}}T^{3})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{P/N_{\mathrm{MC}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>MC</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{P/N_{\mathrm{MC}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{3+\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{3+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;"></th>
<th id="S1.T1.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;">Forward-pass (large but finite-width)</th>
<td id="S1.T1.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(NT^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(NT^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\gamma}/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\gamma}/N</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{2+\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{2+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;">Rich Mean-Field Scaling</th>
<th id="S1.T1.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;">Forward-pass (large but finite-width)</th>
<td id="S1.T1.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(NT^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(NT^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\gamma}/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\gamma}/N</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-bottom: 4.30554pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{2+\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{2+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S1.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Summary of infinite-width transformer inference-time complexity dependent on the context length <math id="S1.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>; the number of training data points <math id="S1.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>; the number of Monte Carlo samples <math id="S1.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{MC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>MC</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{MC}}</annotation></semantics></math>; the width of the estimating finite transformer <math id="S1.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>; a <math id="S1.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-independent constant <math id="S1.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>; and one can take any <math id="S1.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. For the heuristic complexity class EPTHS, see Sec. <a href="#S3" title="3 Setting and Definitions ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our key contributions are:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Formal Definition of Algorithmic Learning:</span> We provide a verifiable definition of what it means for a neural network to <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">capture an algorithm</span>. This definition, stated formally below, involves an input measure that is scalable across problem sizes (<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>), an arbitrary but finite sample budget <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> for obtaining the correct problem instance output with probability (<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>) on instances up to size <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a small logarithmic budget (<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log(T/T_{0}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log(T/T_{0}))</annotation></semantics></math>) for fine-tuning on larger instance sizes (<math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>). This logarithmic allowance is provided solely to correct for architectural non-idealities, such as attention dilution and positional encoding drift, rather than to learn the task logic itself.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Examples of Captured and Non-Captured Algorithms:</span> We show a transformer that captures the induction head search-like algorithm and sorting. Conversely, we show several transformers, including very deep ones, that fail to capture the source-target shortest path problem (SPP) problem and Max Flow / Minimal Cut (MinCut) problem.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                We comment that for our choice of critical graphs, we obtain sparse connectivity, implying that SPP can be solved in <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T)</annotation></semantics></math> compute.</span></span></span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Upper Complexity Bounds:</span> We show that, while performing a forward-pass on a finite-depth, infinite-width transformer requires infinite compute, the predictor can be evaluated using only <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(PdT^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(PdT^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> floating-point operations (FLOPs) on a serial machine (with <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> data samples of <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> input dimension). As a corollary, we prove that an infinite-width transformer, despite being expressive enough to represent arbitrarily complex functions, cannot capture an algorithm of heuristic complexity beyond <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{3+\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{3+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>. Further making the reasonable assumption that transformers converge to their rich or lazy infinite-width limits when the number of heads and internal dimensions scale as <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, for some architecture-dependent exponent <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we show that both lazy and rich transformer networks cannot capture an algorithm of heuristic complexity beyond <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{2+\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{2+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This suggests that, while feature learning can have drastic sample complexity effects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bietti et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghorbani et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, it does not have similar inference complexity effects. Our results are summarized in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we comment that our main purpose here is to present a framework in which computational complexity theory, OOD inductive bias of transformers, and shortcut learning can be sharply defined and contrasted. In particular, further refinement to the inductive bias we find towards lower complexity functions is needed to explain some of our numerical results, in particular those on the SPP.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work intersects with several active areas of deep learning and complexity theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Grokking and Algorithmic Reasoning:</span>
The phenomenon of “grokking” – where generalization emerges long after overfitting – has been widely observed in math tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Power et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barak et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nanda et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. However, these tasks have <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log(n)),O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log(n)),O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> computational complexity and, more crucially, experiments were carried out on specific instance sizes, rendering computational complexity ill-defined.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kolmogorov and Sample Complexity.</span> Prior work in algorithmic information theory suggests that neural networks are strongly biased towards functions with low Kolmogorov complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Valle-Pérez et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dingle et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. This is a different notion of complexity, which, for functions representing an algorithm, does not scale with input size. In addition, various works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bietti et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghorbani et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2019</a>)</cite> studied the effect of feature learning on sample complexity. However, functions which are hard to learn are not necessarily hard to compute (e.g. <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-sparse parity).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Worst-case Expressivity / Circuit Complexity.</span> A complementary line of work studies transformers as constant-depth threshold circuits, placing them in <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{TC}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝖳𝖢</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{TC}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrill et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a></cite> established an unconditional lower bound for multi-layer decoder-only transformers. In contrast, our perspective is “softer”. We analyze average-case behavior and learnability via infinite-width limits (NNGP/NTK), where success is approximate and often stated in high probability, and where complexity is tied to inference cost / kernel evaluation rather than to exact worst-case computation. In the limit, we have universal expressivity, and thus focus on inductive bias, whereas the aforementioned literature addresses limits to expressivity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithmic Alignment and Graph Neural Networks (GNN).</span> Prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Veličković et al., 2020</a>)</cite> has argued that successful algorithmic extrapolation requires the network’s architecture to be structurally isomorphic to the target algorithm’s dynamic programming steps (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abbe et al. (2024)</a></cite>). This is consistent with our numerical findings for induction heads and sorting, which are natural for a transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weiss et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. However, these works primarily focus on bounds for in-distribution sample complexity and provide strong empirical evidence that algorithmic alignment is sufficient for good OOD generalization (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite>). They also work with strong step-by-step supervision. In contrast, our work offers a sharp definition for algorithmic capture with standard supervision, links it directly to inference-time complexity, and derives a formal “unlearnability” result, proving that, despite our networks’ universal expressivity, algorithms exceeding certain (heuristic) complexity cannot be learned regardless of alignment. Finally, we note that preliminary experiments we carried out on GNNs showed good generalization in-distribution but failed to capture the algorithm under standard supervised training.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11161v1/induction_sorting_ens40.svg" id="S2.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/188;" width="476" height="188"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Induction and Sorting Tasks</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11161v1/SPP_ShallowNDeep.svg" id="S2.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/344;" width="476" height="344"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SPP Shallow vs Deep</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11161v1/MinCut_ShallowDeep.svg" id="S2.F1.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:486/338;" width="486" height="338"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mincut Shallow vs Deep</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Empirical Verification of Algorithmic Capture.<span id="S2.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
We train models on problem instances of size <math id="S2.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> reaching accuracy <math id="S2.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> after seeing <math id="S2.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> data points. We then fine-tune these models on larger instance sizes (<math id="S2.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>), re-achieving <math id="S2.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-accuracy after seeing <math id="S2.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> extra datapoints.
Dots are average empirical values based on <math id="S2.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S2.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> transformer networks, and solid lines are best fits to <math id="S2.F1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\log(T/T_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\log(T/T_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> meant to guide the eye. For induction and sorting, <math id="S2.F1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> appears bounded by a logarithmic growth, suggesting algorithmic capture. However, for Shortest Path and Minimal Cut, both very deep (<math id="S2.F1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> layers) and standard (<math id="S2.F1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> layers) architectures exhibit a superlinear growth. For further experimental details see App. <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Further Experimental Details ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Setting and Definitions</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider supervised learning of optimization algorithms with discrete outputs having a minimal spacing of <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, namely, the distance between any two possible outputs <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume a family of probability measures <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mu_{X,T}\}_{T\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mu_{X,T}\}_{T\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> on the instance space at size <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, from which we can efficiently sample; when appropriate (e.g., for graph problems), <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen in the critical regime (e.g., large clusters).
By convention, we use <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote an input data-point and <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>, to denote its embedded tokens. Then <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the input sequence length and <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> the input dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a transformer can <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">capture an algorithm</span> <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math> at sufficiently small accuracy <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> if there exist <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}=T_{0}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}=T_{0}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=C(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=C(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}=P_{0}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}=P_{0}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the transformer will return <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(X)</annotation></semantics></math> (up to <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta/3</annotation></semantics></math>) for every <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with probability at least <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> over a test draw <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>
when trained using the following two-stage protocol:
(1) initial training on <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
samples from <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,1},\dots,\mu_{X,T_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,1},\dots,\mu_{X,T_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>;
(2) secondary fine-tuning using
<math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\log(T/T_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\log(T/T_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
additional samples from <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,1},\dots,\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,1},\dots,\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Alternatively, we allow <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> separate fine-tuning stages with <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}&lt;T_{1}&lt;T_{2}\dots&lt;T_{M}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}&lt;T_{1}&lt;T_{2}\dots&lt;T_{M}=T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\log(T_{m}/T_{m-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\log(T_{m}/T_{m-1})</annotation></semantics></math> additional data-points sampled from <math id="S3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,1},\dots,\mu_{X,T_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,1},\dots,\mu_{X,T_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The fine-tuning stage(s) are necessary to avoid trivial pitfalls, such as attention dilution. In a Bayesian context, we simply allow <math id="S3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> datapoints from <math id="S3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,1},\ldots,\mu_{X,T_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,1},\ldots,\mu_{X,T_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> and additional <math id="S3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\log(T/T_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\log(T/T_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> points from <math id="S3.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,1},\ldots,\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,1},\ldots,\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> demonstrates two instances of algorithmic capture and two instances of its failure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We view such trained transformers through the prism of heuristic complexity classes and distributional problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bogdanov and Trevisan, 2006</a>)</cite>.
An algorithm <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an <em id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">efficient polynomial-time heuristic scheme</em> (EPTHS) of complexity <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> for a distributional task under <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math> if for every <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> there exists an implementation <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\delta}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\delta}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> returns a <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta/3</annotation></semantics></math>-accurate value for the algorithm’s task in time <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\eta(1/\delta)T^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\eta(1/\delta)T^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> with probability at least <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          By analogy to an efficient polynomial-time approximation scheme (EPTAS), but swapping guarantees: EPTAS gives (<math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>)-solutions for all inputs while EPTHS gives exact solutions for most inputs (w.h.p. over <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></span></span>
In principle, <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is allowed to be exponential (or worse) in <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Average-case polynomial-time complexity of an algorithmic task establishes an upper bound for EPTHS: if an algorithm <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> returns a correct value <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math> in time <math id="S3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and if <math id="S3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}[t(X)]=O(T^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}[t(X)]=O(T^{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> establishes an EPTHS of complexity <math id="S3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{k})</annotation></semantics></math> for the distributional task under <math id="S3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Markov’s inequality</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Pr\nolimits_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[t(X)\geq\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}[t(X)]/\delta\right]\leq\delta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pr\nolimits_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[t(X)\geq\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}[t(X)]/\delta\right]\leq\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Then, as a <math id="S3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> implementation, <math id="S3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, run <math id="S3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> on an instance <math id="S3.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for up to <math id="S3.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}[t(X)]/\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}[t(X)]/\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> steps. If <math id="S3.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> finished within the allotted step budget, provide its output, otherwise, output a random value for the task. By construction, <math id="S3.p3.m27" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Pr_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[\left\lvert A_{\delta}(X)-A(X)\right\rvert=0\right]&gt;1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[\left\lvert A_{\delta}(X)-A(X)\right\rvert=0\right]&gt;1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in time <math id="S3.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{k}/\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{k}/\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is correct.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we will argue that, if a transformer captured an algorithm, its kernel predictor is an EPTHS w.r.t. the algorithm’s task with <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and down to <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on assumptions, for any <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, if the inference-time heuristic computational complexity of the transformer scales weaker than the target algorithm heuristic complexity, the transformer will not be able to capture that algorithm. Part of this work is dedicated to bounding this inference-time compute for transformers trained using the above two-stage protocol.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Architecture-wise, we focus on decoder-only transformers with softmax self-attention and no causal masking. Our inputs <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> will represent problem instances of size <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g., computing the length of the shortest path from a specific source to a specific target node. For problems in which all sequence indices in <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> are interchangeable, apart from <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> special tokens (e.g. those representing source and target nodes in graph problems), we encode such large symmetry using positional encoding that only distinguishes between special tokens and the rest. For instance, for the SPP, the actual input to the transformer will be <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by concatenating one-dimensional vectors to rows of the SPP instance representation <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1,T</annotation></semantics></math> (as they play symmetric roles) and <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0]</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Low Complexity Bias of Lazy Learning</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finite networks have inference-time complexity bounded by the number of FLOPs needed for their forward-pass, which, for transformers, scales as <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (see App. <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Inference-time Complexity ‣ Appendix A Transformers in Detail ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>), and, thus, finite transformers cannot express functions that require more than <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> computational steps to evaluate. Infinitely-wide networks, however, are universal approximators and, thus, expressivity-wise, have unbounded complexity. Still, their inductive biases may very well bias them towards low computational complexity solutions. To this end, we seek to analyze and upper bound the inference-time complexity of a trained transformer in the extreme over-parameterized limit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hron et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>The Infinite-width Limit</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In a suitable limit of infinite width, deep neural networks initialized with standard scaling evolve linearly in their parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hron et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacot et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. The network predictor <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)</annotation></semantics></math> converges to a kernel predictor defined by the NTK. The kernel predictor takes the form of a weighted sum over the training dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{\nu}\}_{\nu=1}^{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{\nu}\}_{\nu=1}^{P}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{K}(X_{*})=\sum_{\nu=1}^{P}\alpha_{\nu}K(X_{*},X_{\nu})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{K}(X_{*})=\sum_{\nu=1}^{P}\alpha_{\nu}K(X_{*},X_{\nu}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, instead of performing a forward-pass through the network, we consider the computation complexity of evaluating Eq. (<a href="#S4.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 4.1 The Infinite-width Limit ‣ 4 Low Complexity Bias of Lazy Learning ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>): it is the product of the circuit size <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
and the architectural cost of evaluating <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            As we are evaluating inference-time complexity, matrix inversion in <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}=(K+\kappa I_{P})^{-1}\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}=(K+\kappa I_{P})^{-1}\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is not incorporated. We consider it to be a part of training complexity.</span></span></span>
Accordingly, we analyze the number of FLOPs required to compute the kernel entries. Here, the kernel <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the inner product of the inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We note that the two-step learning protocol used in our experiments has a corresponding NTK formulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bennani et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, where the final predictor is the first-step predictor plus the NTK predictor associated with second-step acting on the errors made by the first-step predictor. Finally, we assume <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert\alpha_{\nu}\right\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert\alpha_{\nu}\right\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> are upper bounded by a <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-independent constant, as would be the typical case. We note that our proof below also accommodates an <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sqrt{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>P</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sqrt{P})</annotation></semantics></math> bound for <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lvert\alpha_{\nu}\right\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lvert\alpha_{\nu}\right\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the worst-case for finite ridge.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            By scaling <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{MC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{MC}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined below, by an additional factor of <math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>P</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>The Fully-connected Network</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a warm-up, consider a fully-connected network (FCN) acting on a flattened version of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. The complexity of evaluating the FCN Neural Network Gaussian Process (NNGP) kernel for a sequence of length <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is dominated by the dot product scaling as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(Td)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(Td)</annotation></semantics></math>. Crucially, NNGP kernel recursions are element-wise on the Gram matrix, involving no mixing across sequence indices. Consequently, depth (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>) only adds an additive <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+L</annotation></semantics></math> term to the inference-time computational complexity. Due to various fixed-points which appear in these recursions, the infinite-depth limit is also likely to be of finite computational complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schoenholz et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. Next, we turn to the more interesting case of the transformer, where sequence indices interact in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho and Saul (2009)</a></cite> recursion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Kernel Propagation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn to transformer networks (see App. <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Setup ‣ Appendix A Transformers in Detail ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> for a detailed setup) whose infinite width / attention limit was analyzed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hron et al. (2020)</a></cite>.
The sufficient statistic (at infinite width) describing the transformer block’s state in the NNGP limit is the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">token-to-token covariance matrix</span> <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}^{\prime}(X_{1},X_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}^{\prime}(X_{1},X_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> between inputs <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1},X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1},X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Sigma}^{\prime}_{ab}(X_{1},X_{2})=\\
\lim_{d_{\text{model}}\to\infty}\frac{1}{d_{\text{model}}}\mathbb{E}\left[\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}(X_{1})^{\top}\bm{z^{\prime}}_{b}(X_{2})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>b</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Sigma}^{\prime}_{ab}(X_{1},X_{2})=\\
\lim_{d_{\text{model}}\to\infty}\frac{1}{d_{\text{model}}}\mathbb{E}\left[\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}(X_{1})^{\top}\bm{z^{\prime}}_{b}(X_{2})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> are the outputs of the MLP layer of the previous transformer block, which are multivariate Gaussian at infinity. When considering the first transformer block and working with learned embeddings and positional encoding, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> would be the embedded inputs in the model’s internal representation space. We comment that fixed embedding can be analyzed similarly but requires defining three separate <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z^{\prime}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z^{\prime}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>-like fields for values, keys, and queries. Kernel evaluation corresponds to propagating the token-to-token covariance matrix, in the sense of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho and Saul (2009)</a></cite>, through the transformer block, starting from the attention layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a multi-head attention layer with <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> heads and inputs <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}(X)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}(X)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math> which we package into <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}(X)\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d_{\text{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}(X)\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The attention layer’s output at token <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math> on input <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}_{a}(X)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{H}}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\sum_{c=1}^{T}A^{h}_{ac}(X)W^{h}_{V}\bm{z^{\prime}}_{c}(X)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>H</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>c</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}_{a}(X)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{H}}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\sum_{c=1}^{T}A^{h}_{ac}(X)W^{h}_{V}\bm{z^{\prime}}_{c}(X),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{h}_{ac}(X)=\\
\Softmax\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}(X)^{\top}W^{h}_{Q}(W^{h}_{K})^{\top}Z^{\prime}(X)^{\top}\right)_{c}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>Softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{h}_{ac}(X)=\\
\Softmax\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}(X)^{\top}W^{h}_{Q}(W^{h}_{K})^{\top}Z^{\prime}(X)^{\top}\right)_{c}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">are the attention weights. The covariance update requires computing the expectation in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 4.3 Kernel Propagation ‣ 4 Low Complexity Bias of Lazy Learning ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)
for all pairs <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>. This involves several Gaussian averages over: <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, query / key weights <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{K}^{h},W_{Q}^{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{K}^{h},W_{Q}^{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and value weights <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}^{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times d_{\text{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}^{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The latter is straightforward, yielding</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\bm{z}_{a}(X_{1})^{\top}\bm{z}_{b}(X_{2})|\bm{z^{\prime}}]=\frac{1}{Hd_{\text{model}}}\sum_{h,j=1}^{H,d_{\text{model}}}\\
\mathbb{E}_{W^{h}_{Q},W^{h}_{K}}\left[\sum_{c,e=1}^{T}\frac{e^{S^{h}_{ac}(X_{1})}[\bm{z^{\prime}}_{c}(X_{1})]_{j}}{\sum_{d=1}^{T}e^{S^{h}_{ad}(X_{1})}}\frac{e^{S^{h}_{be}(X_{2})}[\bm{z^{\prime}}_{e}(X_{2})]_{j}}{\sum_{f=1}^{T}e^{S^{h}_{bf}(X_{2})}}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>𝒛</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>c</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>e</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\bm{z}_{a}(X_{1})^{\top}\bm{z}_{b}(X_{2})|\bm{z^{\prime}}]=\frac{1}{Hd_{\text{model}}}\sum_{h,j=1}^{H,d_{\text{model}}}\\
\mathbb{E}_{W^{h}_{Q},W^{h}_{K}}\left[\sum_{c,e=1}^{T}\frac{e^{S^{h}_{ac}(X_{1})}[\bm{z^{\prime}}_{c}(X_{1})]_{j}}{\sum_{d=1}^{T}e^{S^{h}_{ad}(X_{1})}}\frac{e^{S^{h}_{be}(X_{2})}[\bm{z^{\prime}}_{e}(X_{2})]_{j}}{\sum_{f=1}^{T}e^{S^{h}_{bf}(X_{2})}}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The attention scores are given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S^{h}_{ac}(X)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}{\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}}(X)^{\top}W^{h}_{Q}(W^{h}_{K})^{\top}\bm{z^{\prime}}_{c}(X)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>c</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S^{h}_{ac}(X)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}{\bm{z^{\prime}}_{a}}(X)^{\top}W^{h}_{Q}(W^{h}_{K})^{\top}\bm{z^{\prime}}_{c}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.5" class="ltx_p">In the limit <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{model}},d_{k}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{model}},d_{k}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the scores are multivariate zero-centered Gaussians w.r.t. to the ensemble of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{K},W_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{K},W_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> with correlation given by</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{W^{h}_{Q},W^{h}_{K}}\left[S_{ac}^{h}(X_{1})S^{h}_{be}(X_{2})\right|\left.\bm{z^{\prime}}\vphantom{S_{ac}^{h}(X_{1})}\right]=\\
\left(\frac{{\bm{z}^{\prime}_{a}}(X_{1})^{\top}{\bm{z}^{\prime}_{b}}(X_{2})}{d_{\text{model}}}\right)\left(\frac{\bm{z^{\prime}}_{c}(X_{1})^{\top}\bm{z^{\prime}}_{e}(X_{2})}{d_{\text{model}}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>c</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo><mi>e</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{W^{h}_{Q},W^{h}_{K}}\left[S_{ac}^{h}(X_{1})S^{h}_{be}(X_{2})\right|\left.\bm{z^{\prime}}\vphantom{S_{ac}^{h}(X_{1})}\right]=\\
\left(\frac{{\bm{z}^{\prime}_{a}}(X_{1})^{\top}{\bm{z}^{\prime}_{b}}(X_{2})}{d_{\text{model}}}\right)\left(\frac{\bm{z^{\prime}}_{c}(X_{1})^{\top}\bm{z^{\prime}}_{e}(X_{2})}{d_{\text{model}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.6" class="ltx_p">In the limit, the overlap concentrates to the average, being the following kernel</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{d_{\text{model}}\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}_{W^{h}_{Q},W^{h}_{K}}\left[S_{ac}^{h}(X_{1})S^{h}_{be}(X_{2})\right|\left.\bm{z^{\prime}}\vphantom{S_{ac}^{h}(X_{1})}\right]\right]\\
=\Sigma^{\prime}_{ab}(X_{1},X_{2})\Sigma^{\prime}_{ce}(X_{1},X_{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>S</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">′</mo></msup><mo>]</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{d_{\text{model}}\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}_{W^{h}_{Q},W^{h}_{K}}\left[S_{ac}^{h}(X_{1})S^{h}_{be}(X_{2})\right|\left.\bm{z^{\prime}}\vphantom{S_{ac}^{h}(X_{1})}\right]\right]\\
=\Sigma^{\prime}_{ab}(X_{1},X_{2})\Sigma^{\prime}_{ce}(X_{1},X_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Complexity and Accuracy of Kernel Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the computational complexity of evaluating Eq. (<a href="#S4.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 4.3 Kernel Propagation ‣ 4 Low Complexity Bias of Lazy Learning ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) using Monte Carlo. One efficient approach proceeds as follows. First, we draw <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2T^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2T^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> score samples, according to their multivariate Gaussian distribution. Utilizing the tensor product structure in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 4.3 Kernel Propagation ‣ 4 Low Complexity Bias of Lazy Learning ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), this can be done at <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> computational cost instead of the native <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{6})</annotation></semantics></math> (see App. <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Complexity of Score Draws ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>). Producing the attention matrices <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(X_{1}),A(X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(X_{1}),A(X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> then requires additional <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> steps. Finally, replacing <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{model}}^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^{d_{\text{model}}}{\bm{z}^{\prime}_{c}(X_{1})}_{j}{\bm{z}^{\prime}_{e}(X_{2})}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{model}}^{-1}\sum_{j=1}^{d_{\text{model}}}{\bm{z}^{\prime}_{c}(X_{1})}_{j}{\bm{z}^{\prime}_{e}(X_{2})}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}_{ce}(X_{1},X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}_{ce}(X_{1},X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, Eq. (<a href="#S4.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 4.3 Kernel Propagation ‣ 4 Low Complexity Bias of Lazy Learning ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) can be written in matrix form <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(X_{1})\Sigma^{\prime}(X_{1},X_{2})A(X_{2})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(X_{1})\Sigma^{\prime}(X_{1},X_{2})A(X_{2})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating additional <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> operations.
The cost of one MC evaluation is thus <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>, dominated by matrix multiplication and efficient Cholesky decomposition. Assuming, for simplicity, that this attention layer is the final transformer block, the overall cost of estimating the NNGP kernel predictor becomes <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(PN_{\mathrm{MC}}T^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>MC</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(PN_{\mathrm{MC}}T^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> with MC-induced noise that scales as <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sqrt{P/N_{\mathrm{MC}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>MC</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sqrt{P/N_{\mathrm{MC}}})</annotation></semantics></math> for a given <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Considering Eq. (<a href="#S4.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 4.1 The Infinite-width Limit ‣ 4 Low Complexity Bias of Lazy Learning ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we note that <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜶</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a property of the training set, agnostic of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> at inference. Hence, its magnitude does not scale with <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (unless <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> does). Furthermore, the doubled attention factor being averaged over is bounded in a <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-independent manner due to the layer-norm modules (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{c,e}\{\bm{z}^{\prime}_{c}(X_{1})^{\top}\bm{z}^{\prime}_{e}(X_{2})\}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{c,e}\{\bm{z}^{\prime}_{c}(X_{1})^{\top}\bm{z}^{\prime}_{e}(X_{2})\}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>). And since the MC-induced noise on the kernel predictor scales as <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sqrt{P/N_{\mathrm{MC}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>MC</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sqrt{P/N_{\mathrm{MC}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be reduced to the desired <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-accuracy of the outputs, independent of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. As further layers of the transformer block (e.g., MLP) do not mix sequence indices, propagating <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> through those only requires its diagonal elements and has lower complexity (see App. <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Kernel Propagation ‣ Appendix A Transformers in Detail ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In App. <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Lipschitz Constant of the MLP and Attention Layer Recursions ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> we argue that this scaling of error and compute extend to the full transformer. The crucial observation is that, while MC error can accumulate across different layers by yielding noisy versions of the true layer-wise <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, this accumulation effect is <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> independent, since the kernel recursion relations of the attention layer and MLP layer have a <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-independent Lipschitz constant. Thus, to maintain constant accuracy, one only needs to scale the number of MC samples with depth (<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>), not with context length (<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Assume now that a transformer in the infinite width / attention limit has captured an algorithm <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for sufficiently small <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}=T_{0}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}=T_{0}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=C(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=C(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=P_{0}(\delta)+C\log(T/T_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=P_{0}(\delta)+C\log(T/T_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the kernel predictor <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> from Eq. (<a href="#S4.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 4.1 The Infinite-width Limit ‣ 4 Low Complexity Bias of Lazy Learning ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) satisfies</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Pr\nolimits_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[\left\lvert f_{\delta}(X)-A(X)\right\rvert&lt;\Delta/3\right]\geq 1-\delta/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pr\nolimits_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[\left\lvert f_{\delta}(X)-A(X)\right\rvert&lt;\Delta/3\right]\geq 1-\delta/2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">for any <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using MC to evaluate <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\delta}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\delta}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\delta}^{\mathrm{MC}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi><mi>MC</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\delta}^{\mathrm{MC}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the MC-induced noise scale, then by the sub-Gaussian tail bound, we have</p>
<table id="S4.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\nolimits_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[\left\lvert f_{\delta}^{\mathrm{MC}}(X)-f_{\delta}(X)\right\rvert&lt;\Delta/6\right]\geq\\
1-2e^{-c\Delta^{2}N_{\mathrm{MC}}/P}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>δ</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>MC</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>≥</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>MC</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\nolimits_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[\left\lvert f_{\delta}^{\mathrm{MC}}(X)-f_{\delta}(X)\right\rvert&lt;\Delta/6\right]\geq\\
1-2e^{-c\Delta^{2}N_{\mathrm{MC}}/P}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Applying the triangle inequality, we have</p>
<table id="S4.SS4.p4.5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\nolimits_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[\left\lvert f_{\delta}^{\mathrm{MC}}(X)-A(X)\right\rvert&lt;\Delta/2\right]\geq\\
1-2e^{-c\Delta^{2}N_{\mathrm{MC}}/P}-\delta/2," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>δ</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>MC</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>]</mo><mo>≥</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>MC</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\nolimits_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[\left\lvert f_{\delta}^{\mathrm{MC}}(X)-A(X)\right\rvert&lt;\Delta/2\right]\geq\\
1-2e^{-c\Delta^{2}N_{\mathrm{MC}}/P}-\delta/2,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.6" class="ltx_p">and we want <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2e^{-c\Delta^{2}N_{\mathrm{MC}}/P}\approx\delta/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>MC</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2e^{-c\Delta^{2}N_{\mathrm{MC}}/P}\approx\delta/2</annotation></semantics></math>, which imposes <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{MC}}\propto P\log(1/\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>MC</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{MC}}\propto P\log(1/\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Evaluating <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\delta}^{\mathrm{MC}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi><mi>MC</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\delta}^{\mathrm{MC}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, as we have argued, requires <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(PN_{\mathrm{MC}}{T^{3}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>MC</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(PN_{\mathrm{MC}}{T^{3}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the infinite width / attention transformer is an EPTHS for the task defined by the algorithm it captured with complexity <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{3+\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{3+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (coming from <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(T)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(T)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) w.r.t. <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-implementation is given by <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\delta}^{\mathrm{MC}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>δ</mi><mi>MC</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\delta}^{\mathrm{MC}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, computed in time <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(P^{2}\log(1/\delta){T^{3}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(P^{2}\log(1/\delta){T^{3}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta/2</annotation></semantics></math> provides a conclusive decision rule.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Tighter Bounds and Feature Learning</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The above results invite several extensions both in terms of tightness and richness, which we explore here.
Unlike in FCNs, kernel evaluation for transformers is far more costly than a forward-pass because it requires tracking correlations over the entire context. In contrast, various bounds on NTK convergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang and Yau, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacot et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2021</a>)</cite> suggest that, at least for MLPs and CNNs, the discrepancy between the NTK kernel predictor and the finite network scales as <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\gamma}/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\gamma}/N</annotation></semantics></math> for some architecture-dependent constant exponent <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the architecture “width” or redundancy scale (e.g., width for MLPs, channels for convolutional neural networks, or heads and internal dimensions for transformers).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, however, how well this scaling holds in extreme OOD cases such as ours is, to the best of our knowledge, not well explored. In the section below, we provide analytical evidence that it nevertheless holds. Given this, if a transformer captured an algorithm, then <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=O(\log(T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=O(\log(T))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> need only scale poly-logarithmically with <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, we can approximate the infinite-width lazy NTK predictor using a forward-pass on a finite trained network with <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\propto\log(T)^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\propto\log(T)^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, getting computational complexity scaling of <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{2}\log(T)^{\gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{2}\log(T)^{\gamma})</annotation></semantics></math>
(see App. <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Inference-time Complexity ‣ Appendix A Transformers in Detail ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>). This is a tighter upper bound compared to <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PN_{\mathrm{MC}}T^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>MC</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PN_{\mathrm{MC}}T^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> discussed in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Complexity and Accuracy of Kernel Evaluation ‣ 4 Low Complexity Bias of Lazy Learning ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>. We comment that it can be further improved to <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T)</annotation></semantics></math> with rank-limited attention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Turning to the rich learning regime, various works on Bayesian learning view rich learning as lazy learning with adapted pre-activation kernels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rubin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon and Pehlevan, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aitchison, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sompolinsky, 1988</a>)</cite> or weights covariance matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rubin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ménard et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Notably, repeating our previous kernel computation with Gaussian weights having richer covariance bounded by some <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-independent constant does not make kernel assessment more complex, thus maintaining <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{3}\log(T)^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{3}\log(T)^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> complexity. We further note that the emergence of learned circuits, whose number may depend polynomially in <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, again brings us to <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-dependence scaling, as in the forward-pass computation on a finite network.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we can combine these two extensions by approximating the rich infinite-width NTK predictor using a finite neural network under the assumption that the discrepancy scales as <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\gamma}/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\gamma}/N</annotation></semantics></math> (potentially with <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordelon and Pehlevan, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Seroussi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>). If that is the case, complexity is again upper-bounded by that of the forward-pass.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Perturbation Theory Estimate</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we provide analytic support to the claim that, at least in standard scaling, convergence to the infinite NTK transformer requires <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\mathrm{poly}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\mathrm{poly}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> irrespective of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. This complements existing results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang and Yau, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, which assume a fixed input dimension. The crux of the argument detailed below is that, due to the averaging nature of attention, pre-activations, outputs, and gradients do not scale extensively with <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We proceed by adopting a Bayesian viewpoint and, for simplicity, focus only on predicting the <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-th token. We let <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-th output kernel matrix,</p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{\mu\nu}=K(X_{\mu},X_{\nu})\equiv\Sigma^{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{out}}_{TT}(X_{\mu},X_{\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">out</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{\mu\nu}=K(X_{\mu},X_{\nu})\equiv\Sigma^{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{out}}_{TT}(X_{\mu},X_{\nu})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be the ridge parameter / observation noise. The leading order correction, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{U}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{U}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, to the posterior predictor on the <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-th sequence index,</p>
<table id="S5.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(X_{*})=f_{K}(X_{*})+\frac{1}{N}f_{U}(X_{*})+O(N^{-2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(X_{*})=f_{K}(X_{*})+\frac{1}{N}f_{U}(X_{*})+O(N^{-2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">was given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naveh et al., 2021</a>, Eq. (11))</cite> by</p>
<table id="S5.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{f}_{U}(X_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{f}_{U}(X_{*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{6}\tilde{U}_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\mu_{3}}^{*}\left(\tilde{y}_{\mu_{1}}\tilde{y}_{\mu_{2}}\tilde{y}_{\mu_{3}}-3\tilde{K}_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}}^{-1}\tilde{y}_{\mu_{3}}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{6}\tilde{U}_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\mu_{3}}^{*}\left(\tilde{y}_{\mu_{1}}\tilde{y}_{\mu_{2}}\tilde{y}_{\mu_{3}}-3\tilde{K}_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}}^{-1}\tilde{y}_{\mu_{3}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}=(K+\kappa I_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}=(K+\kappa I_{P})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is the 4th-order functional cumulant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naveh et al., 2021</a>)</cite> of the output, we let</p>
<table id="S5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{y}_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{y}_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\tilde{K}_{\mu\nu}^{-1}y_{\nu}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\tilde{K}_{\mu\nu}^{-1}y_{\nu},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{U}_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\mu_{3}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{U}_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\mu_{3}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=U_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\mu_{3}}^{*}-U_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\mu_{3}\mu_{4}}\tilde{K}_{\mu_{4}\nu}^{-1}K^{*}_{\nu}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=U_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\mu_{3}}^{*}-U_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\mu_{3}\mu_{4}}\tilde{K}_{\mu_{4}\nu}^{-1}K^{*}_{\nu},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">and all repeating indices are summed over the training set (i.e., <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=1,\dots,P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=1,\dots,P</annotation></semantics></math>).
An asterisk on the RHS of Eq. (<a href="#S5.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 5.1 Perturbation Theory Estimate ‣ 5 Tighter Bounds and Feature Learning ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) denotes evaluation at a test point <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, namely, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}_{\nu}=K(X_{*},X_{\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}_{\nu}=K(X_{*},X_{\nu})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\mu_{3}}^{*}=U\left(X_{*},X_{\mu_{1}},X_{\mu_{2}},X_{\mu_{3}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mu_{1}\mu_{2}\mu_{3}}^{*}=U\left(X_{*},X_{\mu_{1}},X_{\mu_{2}},X_{\mu_{3}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the computational complexity of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{f}_{U}(X_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{f}_{U}(X_{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, we argue that all its tensors and vectors can be bounded by a constant in <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\mu}=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\mu}=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}^{-1}_{\mu\nu}\leq 1/\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}^{-1}_{\mu\nu}\leq 1/\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> (see App. <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Kernel Bounds ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). Consequently, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}_{\mu}=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}_{\mu}=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mu\nu},K^{*}_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mu\nu},K^{*}_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> are bounded as they correspond to the variance of the outputs, which have <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> bounded Gaussian tails at initialization, irrespective of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, due to this Gaussian boundedness, the <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>th-order cumulant between any four points, including the test point, also obeys <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mu_{1}...\mu_{4}}\propto U^{*}_{\mu_{1}...\mu_{3}}\propto O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mu_{1}...\mu_{4}}\propto U^{*}_{\mu_{1}...\mu_{3}}\propto O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. We further note that <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\tilde{K}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\tilde{K}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) inference operator on the training set, namely, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}=K\tilde{K}^{-1}\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}=K\tilde{K}^{-1}\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}=(y_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}=(y_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}=(f(X_{\mu}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}=(f(X_{\mu}))</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>P</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A crude approximation, which is nevertheless sufficient for our purpose, is to replace all summands by <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> coming from summations over <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\mu_{3}\in[P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\mu_{3}\in[P]</annotation></semantics></math> scaling times <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{4},\nu\in[P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{4},\nu\in[P]</annotation></semantics></math>, totaling <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\propto P^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\propto P^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A tighter bound comes from noting that the latter two summations have a simple interpretation: for fixed <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\mu_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\mu_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{U}^{*}_{\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\mu_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{U}^{*}_{\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\mu_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the average discrepancy when using GPR to predict <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(\cdot,X_{\mu_{1}},X_{\mu_{2}},X_{\mu_{3}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(\cdot,X_{\mu_{1}},X_{\mu_{2}},X_{\mu_{3}})</annotation></semantics></math>. One may then use GPR error estimate bounds to turn this extra <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> dependence into a <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>P</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{P}</annotation></semantics></math> dependence as follows. First, note that, in general, for a function <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in the RKHS, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(X)=\langle g,K(X,\cdot)\rangle_{{\cal H}_{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ℋ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(X)=\langle g,K(X,\cdot)\rangle_{{\cal H}_{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> and, consequently, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|g(X)|\leq\|g\|_{{\cal H}_{K}}\|K(X,\cdot)\|_{{\cal H}_{K}}=\|g\|_{{\cal H}_{K}}K(X,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ℋ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ℋ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ℋ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|g(X)|\leq\|g\|_{{\cal H}_{K}}\|K(X,\cdot)\|_{{\cal H}_{K}}=\|g\|_{{\cal H}_{K}}K(X,X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, replacing <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(X)</annotation></semantics></math> by a GPR predictor, <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{f}_{K}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{f}_{K}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, and viewing <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as a test point, we may use <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{f}_{K}(X)=K(X,\cdot)^{\top}(K+\kappa I_{P})^{-1}\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{f}_{K}(X)=K(X,\cdot)^{\top}(K+\kappa I_{P})^{-1}\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and bound <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{f}_{K}(X)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{f}_{K}(X)|</annotation></semantics></math> by its RKHS norm. For the latter, we observe that</p>
<table id="S5.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{f}_{K}(X)\|^{2}_{{\cal H}_{K}}=\\
\bm{y}^{\top}(K+\kappa I_{P})^{-1}K(K+\kappa I_{P})^{-1}\bm{y}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ℋ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{f}_{K}(X)\|^{2}_{{\cal H}_{K}}=\\
\bm{y}^{\top}(K+\kappa I_{P})^{-1}K(K+\kappa I_{P})^{-1}\bm{y}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The spectral norm of the middle operator is bounded by <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4\kappa)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4\kappa)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (see App. <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Kernel Bounds ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>), and we finally obtain</p>
<table id="S5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|{f}_{K}(X_{*})|&lt;\frac{{\|\bm{y}\|_{2}}}{4\kappa}\cdot K(X_{*},X_{*})=O(\sqrt{P})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>P</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|{f}_{K}(X_{*})|&lt;\frac{{\|\bm{y}\|_{2}}}{4\kappa}\cdot K(X_{*},X_{*})=O(\sqrt{P}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where we assumed properly normalized kernels and target functions. This implies a worst-case <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\sqrt{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>P</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\sqrt{P})</annotation></semantics></math> bound on the discrepancy as stated.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Induction heads.</span> Here we consider a
vocabulary of size <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=1024</annotation></semantics></math> and sequences of length <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. For each sequence, we first generate random tokens and select an arbitrary ‘trigger’ token from the vocabulary. To prevent ambiguity, we strictly enforce that the trigger appears only where intended by replacing any pre-existing random occurrences with the subsequent token in the vocabulary (modulo <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>). We then inject the trigger token at a random index <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{K}\in\{0,\dots,T/2-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{K}\in\{0,\dots,T/2-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and again at the final sequence index <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The tokens are mapped to learned embeddings (<math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>) and augmented with Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE). The objective is to predict the token following the final trigger. To solve this, the transformer must perform an induction head operation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elhage et al., 2021</a>)</cite>: it must locate the previous occurrence of the trigger
token (at index <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>) and copy the token immediately following it (<math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i_{K}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i_{K}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithmic Sorting.</span> Here we consider a vocabulary of integers <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}=\{0,\dots,999\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>999</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}=\{0,\dots,999\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a sequence length <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. For each instance, we generate a sequence <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> distinct integers sampled uniformly without replacement from <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then construct the full sequence by concatenating the unsorted list <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, a special separator token (SEP), and the sorted version of the list <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. The inputs are mapped to learned embeddings and augmented with RoPE positional encodings. The model is trained using a standard causal next-token prediction objective over the concatenated sequence <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[u,\text{SEP},s]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>SEP</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[u,\text{SEP},s]</annotation></semantics></math>. However, we apply a mask to the loss function to ignore predictions on the unsorted prefix <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, calculating the loss only on the generation of the separator and the sorted suffix <math id="S6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, accuracy is averaged over tokens. This task forces the model to implement a sorting algorithm: at each step of the generation phase, it must attend back to the unsorted prefix <math id="S6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to identify and copy the next smallest element that has not been generated yet.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random Geometric Graphs.</span>
We test algorithmic capture on two types of combinatorial graph problems. To that end, we generate random (undirected) graph instances following the Random Geometric Graph (RGG) setup <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Penrose, 2003</a>)</cite>. First, we draw <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T+2</annotation></semantics></math> points <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0,\ldots,T+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0,\ldots,T+1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.i.d. from a uniform measure <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider each <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a node in our sampled RGG. The nodes <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{T+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{T+1}</annotation></semantics></math> take the special role of source and target, respectively. Next, we fix <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and add an edge of weight (or capacity) <math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> between any two nodes <math id="S6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\bm{x}_{a}-\bm{x}_{b}\|_{2}\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\bm{x}_{a}-\bm{x}_{b}\|_{2}\leq r</annotation></semantics></math>. Unless stated otherwise, we parametrize <math id="S6.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> via the expected degree <math id="S6.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dall and Christensen (2002, Eq. (7))</a></cite> and choose <math id="S6.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> near the regime where a giant linear-sized component emerges. We denote our joint input measure by <math id="S6.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}:=\mu_{\bm{x}}^{\otimes(T+2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}:=\mu_{\bm{x}}^{\otimes(T+2)}</annotation></semantics></math>. From an average-time or heuristic complexity perspective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levin, 1986</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Impagliazzo, 1995</a>)</cite>, this distribution has low sampling complexity but generates relatively hard graph instances.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geometric Planning (SPP).</span>
Consider a finite undirected graph <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math> with node set <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\left\lvert\mathcal{V}\right\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\left\lvert\mathcal{V}\right\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>, edge set <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\left\lvert\mathcal{E}\right\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\left\lvert\mathcal{E}\right\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>, and two distinguished nodes: a source <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and target <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. A (node) path in <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> from source to target is a sequence <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=(v_{0},\ldots,v_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=(v_{0},\ldots,v_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> of nodes such that <math id="S6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i-1},v_{i}\}\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i-1},v_{i}\}\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math> beginning with <math id="S6.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}=v_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}=v_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and ending with <math id="S6.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n}=v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n}=v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Graph weighting constitutes a map <math id="S6.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:\mathcal{E}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:\mathcal{E}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>. In our RGG setting, weighting is constant <math id="S6.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:\mathcal{E}\rightarrow\{1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:\mathcal{E}\rightarrow\{1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The SPP consists of finding an optimal path <math id="S6.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for</p>
<table id="S6.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\pi^{*}\in\argmin_{\begin{subarray}{c}\pi=(v_{0},\ldots,v_{n})\\
v_{0}=v_{s},v_{n}=v_{t}\end{subarray}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c\left(\{v_{i-1},v_{i}\}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">∈</mo><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">argmin</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi^{*}\in\argmin_{\begin{subarray}{c}\pi=(v_{0},\ldots,v_{n})\\
v_{0}=v_{s},v_{n}=v_{t}\end{subarray}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c\left(\{v_{i-1},v_{i}\}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For our <math id="S6.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math>, considered at the critical connectivity threshold, average-case time-complexity for source-target SPP is <math id="S6.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[t(X)\right]=\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}[E(X)+V(X)]=O(T\log(T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}\left[t(X)\right]=\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}[E(X)+V(X)]=O(T\log(T))</annotation></semantics></math>, matching standard worst-case complexity for the single-pair SPP on an undirected unit-weight graph using Breadth-First Search (BFS) and critical connectivity expectation <math id="S6.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}[E(X)]=\Theta(T\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mu_{X,T}}[E(X)]=\Theta(T\log T)</annotation></semantics></math>. As noted in Sec. <a href="#S3" title="3 Setting and Definitions ‣ Algorithmic Capture, Computational Complexity, and Inductive Bias of Infinite Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, this places the distributional SPP task in the EPTHS class of complexity <math id="S6.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{1+\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{1+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S6.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S6.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MinCut (MaxFlow).</span>
Starting from the RGG and graph setup above, we adapt it to add directions to edges, i.e., consider <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}\subseteq\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}\subseteq\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. In a MinCut / MaxFlow network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertsekas, 1998</a>, Proposition 3.2)</cite>, nodes, apart from the source and target, are termed relay nodes. In our directed graph adaptation, relay nodes become bi-directional (with equal capacity), all edges incident to the source have <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> as their initial node, and all edges incident to the target have <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as their terminal node as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramamoorthy et al. (2005, Definition 2)</a></cite>.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Equivalently, we may take the complete directed edge set <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}=\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}=\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> (optionally excluding self-loops) and extend
edge capacity <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> by setting <math id="footnote5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(u,v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(u,v)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all directed pairs <math id="footnote5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> not present in the geometric graph; this leaves the MinCut/MaxFlow value unchanged.</span></span></span> A cut <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subset\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subset\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> in the graph is a subset containing the source <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{s}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{s}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> whose complement <math id="S6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{S}}=\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{S}}=\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the target <math id="S6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}\in\overline{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}\in\overline{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The MinCut problem consists of finding an optimal cut <math id="S6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for</p>
<table id="S6.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{S}^{*}\in\argmin_{\begin{subarray}{c}\mathcal{S}\subset\mathcal{V}\\
v_{s}\in\mathcal{S}\\
v_{t}\in\overline{\mathcal{S}}\end{subarray}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}(v,\bar{v})\in\mathcal{E}\\
v\in\mathcal{S},\bar{v}\in\overline{\mathcal{S}}\end{subarray}}c\left(v,\bar{v}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">∈</mo><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">argmin</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{S}^{*}\in\argmin_{\begin{subarray}{c}\mathcal{S}\subset\mathcal{V}\\
v_{s}\in\mathcal{S}\\
v_{t}\in\overline{\mathcal{S}}\end{subarray}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}(v,\bar{v})\in\mathcal{E}\\
v\in\mathcal{S},\bar{v}\in\overline{\mathcal{S}}\end{subarray}}c\left(v,\bar{v}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Average-case for MinCut/MaxFlow is <math id="S6.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{O(\mathbb{E}[VE])}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{O(\mathbb{E}[VE])}</annotation></semantics></math>, matching standard worst-case complexity for MaxFlow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Orlin, 2013</a>)</cite>. For similar considerations as in SPP, this places MinCut/MaxFlow in the EPTHS class of complexity <math id="S6.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{2+\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{2+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S6.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{X,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{X,T}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S6.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we moved beyond the question of what transformers can express—which, in the infinite-width limit, is effectively everything—and instead asked what algorithms they can learn to execute. By formally defining Algorithmic Capture as the ability to generalize to arbitrary input sizes (<math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>) with minimal sample adaptation, we offered a distinction between genuine algorithmic learning and statistical interpolation. Our theoretical analysis of infinite-width transformers in both the lazy and rich regimes established a rigorous upper bound on their inference-time computational complexity, effectively limiting them to the class of Efficient Polynomial Time Heuristic Schemes (EPTHS), specifically those with complexity scaling no worse than <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{3+\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{3+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and most likely <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T^{2+\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T^{2+\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our empirical findings illustrate a spectrum of algorithmic capabilities that our framework helps decompose. On the one hand, we observe clear instances of algorithmic capture in tasks like induction heads and sorting, where the algorithmic complexity is low and aligns well with finite circuit solutions and RASP programs. On the other, we find tasks like MinCut, where the algorithmic complexity <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(VE)=O(T^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(VE)=O(T^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> pushes against our bounds. Other tasks seem allowed by our tighter bounds, but nonetheless were not captured, even at large depth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This discrepancy highlights a fruitful avenue for future research: refining the estimate of inference-time complexity. Our first bound relies on a “brute force” propagation of kernel covariances, akin to the recursions of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho and Saul (2009)</a></cite>. While robust, this approach may be overly conservative, leaving room for more clever approximations of the kernel. In fact, we conjecture that the dominant spectral weight of the kernel lies in the space of rather simple permutation-invariant sparse functions.<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          To be published.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, it would be interesting to generalize these results to deeper or recurring architectures. Just like in the current work, the fact that these deeper nets can express complex algorithms does not mean that they would learn them, as various works and our own experiments with deep transformers suggest. At least for lazy learning, where our bound is largely determined by the complexity of kernel evaluation, this amounts to understanding fixed points of the kernel recursion equations, which are simple for MLPs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schoenholz et al., 2017</a>)</cite> but less well explored for transformers. Such results can serve as a stepping stone to analyze whether faithful algorithmic circuits can evolve under standard supervised learning or reinforcement learning techniques.</p>
</div>
</section>
